[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to Performance Testing and Types"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Performance testing is a non-functional software testing technique that determines how the stability, speed, scalability, and responsiveness of an application hold up under a given workload. Unlike functional testing that checks if a feature works, performance testing ensures the system performs well under expected and peak load conditions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why is Performance Testing Important?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Validates system reliability and speed against business requirements."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Identifies bottlenecks before the application reaches production."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prevents catastrophic system crashes during high-traffic events (e.g., Black Friday sales)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Optimizes resource utilization (CPU, memory, network bandwidth)."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Metrics Measured in Performance Testing"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into the types of performance testing, it is crucial to understand the core metrics monitored during execution:"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Response Time: The total time it takes to send a request and receive the first byte or the complete response."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Throughput: The number of requests processed per second or transactions handled by the system."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Concurrency: The number of virtual users executing operations on the application at the exact same moment."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Error Rate: The percentage of failed requests relative to total requests during the test."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Major Types of Performance Testing"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Performance testing encompasses several specialized types, each serving a unique objective."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Load Testing: Evaluates the system's behavior under expected normal and peak load conditions. The goal is to identify performance bottlenecks before production."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stress Testing: Involves testing an application under extreme workloads (beyond normal operational capacity) to observe how and when it breaks and how it recovers."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Endurance Testing (Soak Testing): Determines if the system can sustain a continuous, expected load over a significant period without memory leaks or degradation."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Spike Testing: Measures system reaction to sudden and massive increases or decreases in load."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scalability Testing: Determines the application's effectiveness in "scaling up" to support an increased user load."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Load Testing Script Example"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is a simple JavaScript configuration using k6, a modern open-source load testing tool, to simulate virtual users hitting an endpoint."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  vus: 10,
  duration: '30s',
};

export default function () {
  const res = http.get('https://test-api.k6.io/public/crocodiles/');
  check(res, {
    'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200,
  });
  sleep(1);
}"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Performance testing is an indispensable part of the software development lifecycle. By understanding the distinct types of performance tests and leveraging tools like k6, JMeter, or Gatling, engineering teams can proactively ensure their systems deliver a fast, reliable, and scalable user experience."}]
